      "description": "Understand Iran’s general higher-education ministry, its relationship with medical education, university funding, and the limits of available evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "institutional-scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A ministry within a divided education system",
        "content": "Iran’s Ministry of Science, Research and Technology, commonly abbreviated MSRT, belongs to a higher-education system whose responsibilities are divided among institutions. UNESCO’s education profile places basic sciences, humanities, engineering, arts and agriculture within its sphere, while medical universities and clinical education fall under the Ministry of Health and Medical Education. The distinction matters when interpreting a university’s affiliation or an announcement described simply as an Iranian higher-education policy. [s1]\n\nSchool education has a different institutional home: UNESCO identifies the Ministry of Education as responsible for state and non-state education from pre-primary through secondary levels. Consequently, MSRT should not be treated as a single ministry directing every stage and subject of Iranian education. An institution’s educational level, subject area and affiliation all matter. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "financing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "University funding and student support",
        "content": "UNESCO’s financing profile describes MSRT as allocating and overseeing funds for general universities, alongside a separate medical-university financing channel through the health ministry. It also describes government scholarships administered through MSRT, distinguishing support covering tuition and a stipend from support covering tuition alone. These descriptions establish funding arrangements; they do not establish that every institution or applicant receives equivalent support. [s2]\n\nThe same profile describes higher education as partly subsidized rather than universally tuition-free. For international readers, this is a useful correction to the assumption that public responsibility automatically means education without charges. Institution-specific fees and scholarship eligibility require separate verification. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "rights-and-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "University governance and freedom of expression",
        "content": "Administrative responsibilities do not by themselves establish the conditions in which students study or researchers work. Human Rights Watch’s World Report 2025 documented university disciplinary measures against students for peaceful expression during its reporting period. That evidence concerns university governance in practice and should be read alongside institutional accounts of education administration. It does not establish that MSRT personally directed every documented disciplinary decision. [s3]\n\nA careful assessment therefore needs both institutional evidence and evidence from affected students and staff. Funding, academic provision and freedom of expression are distinct questions; progress or formal commitments in one area cannot be used as proof of outcomes in another.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-the-record",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What remains to be established",
        "content": "This account establishes the ministry’s place within a divided education system, but it is not a complete description of its legal powers. A publication-ready institutional profile still requires an inspected mandate, a current organizational chart and documentation explaining the ministry’s relationships with research institutes and technology organizations. The ministry’s English website could not be retrieved during this research. No current officeholder, contact details or organizational performance claim is therefore presented.",
