      "title": "Iran’s Plan and Budget Organization | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the distinction between Iran’s development planning, budget preparation, parliamentary approval and expenditure implementation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "planning-and-budget",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connecting priorities with public spending",
        "content": "The Plan and Budget Organization, abbreviated PBO, occupies the junction between development priorities and annual spending decisions. UNESCO’s higher-education financing profile identifies it as the organization preparing the national budget, which Parliament approves. University funding provides a concrete example of why this coordinating role matters: sector institutions depend on decisions made within a wider allocation process. [s1]\n\nPreparing a budget is analytically different from approving it, releasing funds or delivering a service. Keeping those stages separate helps readers assess whether a government announcement describes a proposal, legal authorization or expenditure that actually occurred.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-framework",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The documented planning framework",
        "content": "A World Bank assessment published in November 2005 describes the Management and Planning Organization, or MPO, as preparing development plans in consultation with sector ministries and universities. Its budget-process table separates preparation by the planning organization from Cabinet approval, parliamentary appropriation and implementation by executive bodies. This is historical evidence about the planning machinery, rather than confirmation that every procedure remains unchanged. [s2]\n\nThe report traces the first substantial plan and planning organization to 1949. It also describes agency performance reporting as part of the relationship between development plans and budget requests. The important institutional distinction is between setting a coordinated framework and carrying out individual programs. A central planning body can review proposals without itself operating each school, hospital or infrastructure project. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "institutional-boundaries",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Planning, treasury and audit",
        "content": "Encyclopaedia Iranica’s historical account distinguishes PBO’s work on plans and budgets from revenue collection and treasury functions undertaken by the Ministry of Economic Affairs and Finance. It also describes parliamentary audit as a separate stage. These distinctions help explain why a single budget document involves several institutions with different responsibilities. [s3]\n\nThat account discusses PBO oversight of development budgets and project completion. Such responsibilities should not be read as evidence that all projects were completed or that spending achieved its intended benefits. Monitoring arrangements describe how performance is supposed to be examined; an assessment of results requires expenditure records, implementation reports and independent scrutiny. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evidence-and-interpretation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading budget claims carefully",
        "content": "The inspected evidence supports an introductory account of budget coordination, but not a current procedural manual. In particular, the historical World Bank report uses the MPO name, while UNESCO’s more recent profile uses PBO. Those documents alone do not establish a complete chronology of reorganizations. [s1][s2]\n\nFor a particular spending claim, the next research step is to identify the relevant budget year and distinguish the proposed amount, enacted allocation and reported expenditure. Until those records are inspected, neither a development target nor a budget announcement establishes completed implementation.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
