    "description": "Iran’s judicial and legal institutions include courts, administrative review bodies and the legal profession. Understanding them requires distinguishing constitutional responsibilities, the jurisdiction of particular bodies and documented conditions of access to justice.",
    "tags": ["Judiciary", "Courts", "Legal institutions", "Administrative justice", "Legal profession", "Due process"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T23:31:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T23:31:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Judicial and Legal Institutions in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to Iran’s courts, judicial administration, administrative review and legal profession, distinguishing constitutional rules from documented practice."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "constitutional-framework",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Judicial authority and administration",
        "content": "Iran’s Constitution describes the judiciary as an independent power, while providing for the Supreme Leader to appoint its head. Article 158 assigns that head responsibility for judicial organization, judicial bills and the administration of judges. Constitutional independence is therefore a formal description that must be examined alongside appointment arrangements and documented practice. [s1]\n\nThe Minister of Justice has a different constitutional function: managing relations between the judiciary and the executive and legislative branches. The minister should not be confused with the head of the judiciary. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "court-jurisdiction",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different courts answer different questions",
        "content": "The GlobaLex legal-system guide distinguishes general courts from courts with specific jurisdiction. Its account explains that general jurisdiction is subject to exceptions established for particular courts. It also distinguishes civil and criminal proceedings and describes appellate review. This is a useful starting point for understanding why the nature of a dispute matters before identifying the appropriate institution. [s2]\n\nAn appeal should not be assumed to follow one identical route in every case. The guide separately discusses criminal courts, the Supreme Court and administrative adjudication. Readers need to establish the type of proceeding and the applicable legislation before interpreting a reference to review by a higher court. The guide was published in 2022 and cannot by itself establish later procedural changes. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "constitutional-functions",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Selected constitutional relationships",
