      {
        "id": "review-and-rights",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Review, reasons and legal representation",
        "content": "The Constitution gives the Supreme Court a role in supervising lawful adjudication and ensuring uniform judicial practice. It separately provides for the Administrative Justice Court to examine complaints against governmental officials, units and regulations. Administrative review is thus a distinct constitutional function. [s1]\n\nConstitutional safeguards also include reasoned judgments, protection against compelled confessions and a right to legal representation. These provisions establish standards against which proceedings can be assessed; their presence does not establish compliance in an individual case. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "documented-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Formal safeguards and documented violations",
        "content": "Human Rights Watch’s World Report 2025 describes systemic due-process and fair-trial violations in the repression of anti-government protests. It reports that Revolutionary Court judges failed to address allegations of torture and ill-treatment, including in proceedings resulting in death sentences. These findings concern documented practice and qualify any account based solely on constitutional guarantees. [s3]\n\nAt a March 2025 press conference, the UN Independent International Fact-Finding Mission on Iran reported serious fair-trial and due-process violations in the repression of the protests that began in 2022. Its findings included violations affecting children and persecution of lawyers and others acting in solidarity with victims. These are findings of an investigative mission, rather than criminal judgments determining the liability of every implicated individual. [s4]\n\nThe distinction between institutional design and implementation is essential. A diagram can explain a legal responsibility; it cannot demonstrate that a defendant received independent representation, that evidence was lawfully obtained or that a remedy was effective.",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "exploring-the-field",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to explore this subject",
        "content": "The pages in this branch separate general courts, appellate courts, the Supreme Court and administrative justice. They also provide distinct entries for property-registration institutions and bar associations. This organization helps keep adjudication, administrative services and professional representation conceptually separate.\n\nFor any specific legal question, a reliable account needs the relevant legislation, its amendment history and evidence about implementation. This overview does not establish filing requirements, deadlines or available remedies in an individual dispute. Its central purpose is to identify institutional distinctions and the evidence needed to evaluate them."
